Hi,

Looking through my roguelikes archives, I had a look at the official
MSDOS distribution of Hack 1.03 (NetHack's predecessor). In the file
hack103.doc, which is the predecessor of Guidebook.doc in the current
NetHack distribution, the following passage appears somewhere near the
end:

> Finally
>
> Of course, there is much about HACK to discover yourself.  The RUMORS
> file provides helpful clues for playing.  Also, saved games have no
> special protection so you can save a game and make a copy of the
> savefile.  Then if you die trying something risky you can use the
> copy to restart your game from the same place.

It appears that one of the first writers (Don Kneller) of the game *did*
approve of savescumming, and actually suggested it himself in the game
documentation. Of course, the current Dev Team seems to be of a different
opinion in this, and so does the majority of rec.games.roguelike.nethack
readers.

Also, that version of Hack didn't identify your items when you died, thus
not allowing you to copy a save file, start again, die, and identify your
items in this way before restarting the same save file.

And I'm not sure if the 1.03 *UNIX* version allows you to copy save files,
either. If not, the quote above is clearly directed at MSDOS players only,
since you cannot prevent them from save scumming anyway.

In Don Kneller's words:

Good Luck and Happy Hacking!!!!!!!

